WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.010 --> 00:00:03.740
Narrator: This is a shot
from the biblical deluge

00:00:03.740 --> 00:00:04.640
in "Noah."

00:00:04.640 --> 00:00:05.910
It may not look like it,

00:00:05.910 --> 00:00:08.340
but this was filmed entirely at night

00:00:08.340 --> 00:00:10.160
under special balloon lights.

00:00:10.160 --> 00:00:12.850
And it took a massive custom rain machine

00:00:12.850 --> 00:00:17.484
to dump up to 200,000 gallons
of water on set each night.

00:00:17.484 --> 00:00:20.820
All that, just so you can
clearly see the raindrops

00:00:20.820 --> 00:00:22.630
in the final shot.

00:00:22.630 --> 00:00:24.938
Hollywood goes to extreme measures

00:00:24.938 --> 00:00:27.304
to portray the force of natural disasters

00:00:27.304 --> 00:00:29.932
as close to real life as possible,

00:00:29.932 --> 00:00:31.690
from creating seven different types

00:00:31.690 --> 00:00:34.251
of CG dust for a sandstorm

00:00:34.251 --> 00:00:37.985
to obsessively building accurate
replicas of an ancient city

00:00:37.985 --> 00:00:40.459
just to destroy it in a single act.

00:00:40.459 --> 00:00:42.470
Here's what eight apocalyptic movies

00:00:42.470 --> 00:00:44.443
looked like behind the scenes.

00:00:44.443 --> 00:00:45.940
Director: Go!

00:00:49.500 --> 00:00:51.890
Narrator: To make an
earthquake feel visceral,

00:00:51.890 --> 00:00:54.430
it isn't enough to just shake the camera.

00:00:54.430 --> 00:00:57.354
All the characters, buildings,
and objects in a scene

00:00:57.354 --> 00:00:59.750
need to be affected by the same force.

00:00:59.750 --> 00:01:02.750
The effects team took
this literally for "2012,"

00:01:02.750 --> 00:01:06.020
advertised as "the mother
of all disaster movies."

00:01:06.020 --> 00:01:08.640
To show the world being
shaken to its core,

00:01:08.640 --> 00:01:10.800
the filmmakers built sprawling sets

00:01:10.800 --> 00:01:12.633
on top of hydraulic lifts

00:01:12.633 --> 00:01:15.032
so they could shake everything in a scene,

00:01:15.032 --> 00:01:18.257
from individual actors to entire houses.

00:01:18.257 --> 00:01:21.350
The crew went through
500,000 tons of steel

00:01:21.350 --> 00:01:22.900
building the shaky decks,

00:01:22.900 --> 00:01:26.000
some of which measured
several thousand square feet.

00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:29.270
And massive mover rigs
underneath made it possible

00:01:29.270 --> 00:01:32.730
to put cars, trucks,
planes, building facades,

00:01:32.730 --> 00:01:34.746
and palm trees on the same set,

00:01:34.746 --> 00:01:37.329
so everything could react
to an earthquake-like force

00:01:37.329 --> 00:01:39.105
in a consistent way.

00:01:39.105 --> 00:01:41.810
The team did have to
use CGI for this scene,

00:01:41.810 --> 00:01:45.700
where a 10.5-magnitude
earthquake chases John Cusack

00:01:45.700 --> 00:01:48.480
through the crumbling streets of LA.

00:01:48.480 --> 00:01:49.680
Getting the destruction

00:01:49.680 --> 00:01:52.900
and the ocean-wave-rippling
movement the director wanted

00:01:52.900 --> 00:01:54.853
required building Wilshire Boulevard

00:01:54.853 --> 00:01:56.360
from scratch in the computer

00:01:56.360 --> 00:02:00.329
and writing a new volume-breaking
system called Drop.

00:02:00.329 --> 00:02:03.190
The artists could enter a
whole CG building into Drop,

00:02:03.190 --> 00:02:04.929
and the program would cut it into

00:02:04.929 --> 00:02:07.844
as many as 90,000 little shapes.

00:02:07.844 --> 00:02:10.685
But even for this almost
entirely digital sequence,

00:02:10.685 --> 00:02:13.202
the team shot what they could practically.

00:02:13.202 --> 00:02:15.824
Here, the actors were
filmed inside the limousine,

00:02:15.824 --> 00:02:17.660
attached to a gimbal that simulated

00:02:17.660 --> 00:02:19.468
the effects of the quake.

00:02:22.527 --> 00:02:25.878
In "The Impossible,"
filmmakers had to recreate

00:02:25.878 --> 00:02:28.964
the 2004 Indian Ocean tsunami.

00:02:28.964 --> 00:02:32.230
These climactic scenes where
the tsunami slams the coast

00:02:32.230 --> 00:02:34.610
over the course of 10 terrifying minutes

00:02:34.610 --> 00:02:37.530
required millions of
gallons of actual seawater,

00:02:37.530 --> 00:02:39.260
darkened with food coloring and tints

00:02:39.260 --> 00:02:40.940
to make the water look dirty.

00:02:40.940 --> 00:02:42.415
Naomi Watts and Tom Holland

00:02:42.415 --> 00:02:44.481
filmed most of their scenes in Spain

00:02:44.481 --> 00:02:47.810
in an almost 400-foot-long seaside tank,

00:02:47.810 --> 00:02:49.510
the largest water tank in Europe

00:02:49.510 --> 00:02:51.690
and second largest in the world.

00:02:51.690 --> 00:02:54.110
Inside the tank, the actors sat in baskets

00:02:54.110 --> 00:02:57.040
that moved on rails
pulled with steel cables.

00:02:57.040 --> 00:02:59.150
This system allowed the
crew to control the speed

00:02:59.150 --> 00:03:01.020
at which the actors moved in the water,

00:03:01.020 --> 00:03:03.390
keeping them at the same
speed as the current.

00:03:03.390 --> 00:03:04.950
The film also had to show the water

00:03:04.950 --> 00:03:07.191
slamming the entire resort area,

00:03:07.191 --> 00:03:08.948
pools, palm trees, and all.

00:03:08.948 --> 00:03:09.865
[water crashing]

00:03:09.865 --> 00:03:11.577
Since the real site in Thailand

00:03:11.577 --> 00:03:14.057
didn't look the way it did in 2004,

00:03:14.057 --> 00:03:16.760
the production designers built
a model of the former resort

00:03:16.760 --> 00:03:18.720
at one-third the scale.

00:03:18.720 --> 00:03:20.090
That was the smallest model

00:03:20.090 --> 00:03:22.900
that would interact with
the wave in a realistic way.

00:03:22.900 --> 00:03:25.150
When it was time to film
the water destroying it all,

00:03:25.150 --> 00:03:27.554
the crew had to get it right in one shot.

00:03:27.554 --> 00:03:30.410
To ensure the wave impact looked right,

00:03:30.410 --> 00:03:33.120
the team did test runs
at Edinburgh Designs,

00:03:33.120 --> 00:03:36.000
a company specializing in wave generators.

00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:39.639
The company usually tries to
create a precise, perfect wave,

00:03:39.639 --> 00:03:41.612
but the filmmakers on "The Impossible"

00:03:41.612 --> 00:03:44.540
wanted a wide, ugly, and chaotic one.

00:03:44.540 --> 00:03:45.840
They achieved that messiness

00:03:45.840 --> 00:03:49.200
by rushing 1,000 gallons
of water over the model set

00:03:49.200 --> 00:03:52.470
with the power of eight
F1 racing cars behind it.

00:03:52.470 --> 00:03:55.470
The cinematographer set up
an apparatus of 10 cameras

00:03:55.470 --> 00:03:57.644
to make sure he captured every angle.

00:03:57.644 --> 00:04:00.311
[wave crashing]

00:04:05.994 --> 00:04:08.250
For the dystopian world of "Mad Max,"

00:04:08.250 --> 00:04:10.980
director George Miller
wanted a disaster sequence

00:04:10.980 --> 00:04:12.990
that would feel apocalyptic --

00:04:12.990 --> 00:04:14.970
a toxic sandstorm with twisters

00:04:14.970 --> 00:04:17.940
sucking cars up into the
sky and spitting them out.

00:04:17.940 --> 00:04:20.410
And it was the biggest
VFX sequence in a movie

00:04:20.410 --> 00:04:23.175
known for its practical stunts.

00:04:23.175 --> 00:04:25.592
The crew started by
filming the actual cars

00:04:25.592 --> 00:04:27.670
racing through the desert in Namibia.

00:04:27.670 --> 00:04:29.820
They used this footage
to perfect the layout,

00:04:29.820 --> 00:04:32.785
movement, and scale of the storm effects.

00:04:32.785 --> 00:04:34.470
The practical shots were then relit

00:04:34.470 --> 00:04:36.540
to fit the unique lighting of the storm

00:04:36.540 --> 00:04:38.260
and combined with digital effects,

00:04:38.260 --> 00:04:41.308
swapping in CG cars and drivers as needed.

00:04:41.308 --> 00:04:43.170
They relit these shots of Tom Hardy

00:04:43.170 --> 00:04:45.300
with more firelight to reflect the fuel

00:04:45.300 --> 00:04:47.800
that was leaking from the
car and catching fire.

00:04:47.800 --> 00:04:50.200
To make the cars look like
they were driving faster

00:04:50.200 --> 00:04:51.110
through the storm,

00:04:51.110 --> 00:04:54.420
the VFX team went with a
conceal-and-reveal approach.

00:04:54.420 --> 00:04:56.260
They replaced the ground in many shots

00:04:56.260 --> 00:04:58.640
and added massive digital dust clouds,

00:04:58.640 --> 00:05:01.740
so viewers could only get
occasional glimpses of cars

00:05:01.740 --> 00:05:03.746
as they raced down the desert.

00:05:03.746 --> 00:05:06.970
Any given shot has up to
seven types of CG dust

00:05:06.970 --> 00:05:08.210
coming up from the ground,

00:05:08.210 --> 00:05:10.239
from the lower levels of fine dust

00:05:10.239 --> 00:05:11.540
blowing around the surface

00:05:11.540 --> 00:05:14.370
to lumpy dust that went
up to about mid-car height

00:05:14.370 --> 00:05:17.386
to massive eddies of
dust billowing around.

00:05:20.300 --> 00:05:22.670
For the historical
disaster movie "Pompeii,"

00:05:22.670 --> 00:05:26.020
the filmmakers had to accurately
recreate this ancient city

00:05:26.020 --> 00:05:29.180
and destroy it with just as much accuracy.

00:05:29.180 --> 00:05:31.348
But what we know about
Pompeii is mostly based

00:05:31.348 --> 00:05:33.810
on scientific hypotheses, written records,

00:05:33.810 --> 00:05:35.860
and what remains of the ruins.

00:05:35.860 --> 00:05:38.550
So director Paul W.S.
Anderson and his team

00:05:38.550 --> 00:05:41.770
spent six years researching
and imaging the city,

00:05:41.770 --> 00:05:44.900
including taking a
laser scan of the ruins.

00:05:44.900 --> 00:05:47.100
They used that to build a life-size set

00:05:47.100 --> 00:05:49.320
based on the real dimensions of Pompeii,

00:05:49.320 --> 00:05:53.251
with streets sized precisely
down to the last millimeter.

00:05:53.251 --> 00:05:55.600
The visual effects team
also had to replicate

00:05:55.600 --> 00:05:58.499
the whole set digitally
for the disaster sequences,

00:05:58.499 --> 00:06:02.985
creating over 100 CG buildings
and over 200 CG props.

00:06:02.985 --> 00:06:05.090
All this work, though, had to be destroyed

00:06:05.090 --> 00:06:08.609
when Mount Vesuvius erupts,
collapses buildings,

00:06:08.609 --> 00:06:12.730
triggers a tsunami, and
ultimately wipes out the city.

00:06:12.730 --> 00:06:14.470
For this scene in the crumbling arena,

00:06:14.470 --> 00:06:18.070
the crew filmed 500 extras
inside the actual set,

00:06:18.070 --> 00:06:21.140
then had VFX artists
digitally extend the set

00:06:21.140 --> 00:06:22.874
and double the extras.

00:06:22.874 --> 00:06:25.020
To animate the digital
crowd realistically,

00:06:25.020 --> 00:06:27.610
the artists used mo-cap
footage of stuntpeople

00:06:27.610 --> 00:06:29.690
pretending to escape the wreckage.

00:06:29.690 --> 00:06:31.110
Next there was the tsunami,

00:06:31.110 --> 00:06:33.770
which capsizes a boat in this scene.

00:06:33.770 --> 00:06:35.640
The crew filmed part of that practically,

00:06:35.640 --> 00:06:37.723
using a gimbal as the boat deck.

00:06:37.723 --> 00:06:39.456
Then the city streets.

00:06:39.456 --> 00:06:40.670
For this chariot chase,

00:06:40.670 --> 00:06:43.056
the crew filmed the
horses and stunt doubles

00:06:43.056 --> 00:06:46.440
on a dirt track in front of
400 feet of green screen,

00:06:46.440 --> 00:06:49.016
then used face replacements
to swap the actors

00:06:49.016 --> 00:06:50.870
into the close-up riding shots.

00:06:50.870 --> 00:06:52.385
But the hardest part was replacing

00:06:52.385 --> 00:06:54.601
all the backgrounds with CG sets,

00:06:54.601 --> 00:06:57.664
all of which were getting
pelted with lava bombs.

00:06:57.664 --> 00:06:59.290
Not everything was digital, though.

00:06:59.290 --> 00:07:01.498
The Canadian studio Dynamic Effects

00:07:01.498 --> 00:07:04.390
filmed actual footage of
fireballs and explosions

00:07:04.390 --> 00:07:05.867
along with some practical shots

00:07:05.867 --> 00:07:08.050
of stunt actors being set on fire

00:07:08.050 --> 00:07:10.516
and ash raining down on the city.

00:07:14.270 --> 00:07:16.410
When earthquakes strike in "San Andreas,"

00:07:16.410 --> 00:07:18.080
the earth splits open in ways

00:07:18.080 --> 00:07:20.438
that haven't been seen in real life,

00:07:20.438 --> 00:07:21.621
at least not yet.

00:07:21.621 --> 00:07:24.350
So the filmmakers had to
depict these giant chasms

00:07:24.350 --> 00:07:26.402
with a terrifying realism.

00:07:26.402 --> 00:07:28.890
Older movies might've created
a background like this

00:07:28.890 --> 00:07:30.200
with a matte painting,

00:07:30.200 --> 00:07:31.970
but these environments had to stand up

00:07:31.970 --> 00:07:35.103
at so many different
distances and camera positions

00:07:35.103 --> 00:07:37.290
that creating a matte
painting for every angle

00:07:37.290 --> 00:07:38.580
wasn't practical.

00:07:38.580 --> 00:07:41.470
So they built many detailed
environments digitally,

00:07:41.470 --> 00:07:44.070
including this expanse
of rural California,

00:07:44.070 --> 00:07:47.880
with an 85-foot-wide, 300-foot-deep chasm.

00:07:47.880 --> 00:07:50.900
The VFX artists referenced
a smaller earth crack

00:07:50.900 --> 00:07:53.500
in Sonora, Mexico, to see
how the ground crumbles

00:07:53.500 --> 00:07:55.000
as soil pulls apart.

00:07:55.000 --> 00:07:57.590
They used that to place
the boulders, small rocks,

00:07:57.590 --> 00:07:59.920
and dust crumbling from
the edges of the cliff

00:07:59.920 --> 00:08:02.760
and the layers of plants,
leaf litter, rocks, sticks,

00:08:02.760 --> 00:08:05.616
and tire tracks in the surrounding area.

00:08:05.616 --> 00:08:07.720
But grounding the CG in reality

00:08:07.720 --> 00:08:09.595
also meant going the extra mile

00:08:09.595 --> 00:08:11.903
to place actors in the digital space,

00:08:11.903 --> 00:08:13.828
like in this scene, where falling rocks

00:08:13.828 --> 00:08:17.226
cause a driver to veer off
the road and over a cliff.

00:08:17.226 --> 00:08:18.640
For the most dramatic shots,

00:08:18.640 --> 00:08:21.037
like showing the car
flying through the air,

00:08:21.037 --> 00:08:25.220
the VFX artists swapped in
a partly or entirely CG car,

00:08:25.220 --> 00:08:27.695
along with a digital
double for the driver.

00:08:27.695 --> 00:08:29.790
And for the dramatic rescue that follows,

00:08:29.790 --> 00:08:32.820
the filmmakers built a
special set in Australia

00:08:32.820 --> 00:08:34.800
with a car hanging from a hydraulic rig

00:08:34.800 --> 00:08:36.800
and a helicopter without rotor blades

00:08:36.800 --> 00:08:38.340
hanging above from a crane.

00:08:38.340 --> 00:08:40.490
They then filmed the
actors strapped in place,

00:08:40.490 --> 00:08:43.077
with The Rock doing his own stunts on set.

00:08:43.077 --> 00:08:44.750
Ray: I'm gonna get you out of here, OK?

00:08:44.750 --> 00:08:47.100
Narrator: Everything came
together with the VFX team,

00:08:47.100 --> 00:08:50.140
swapping out the helicopter
for a CG one in some shots

00:08:50.140 --> 00:08:53.491
or adding digital rotors
and exhaust fumes in others.

00:08:56.137 --> 00:08:58.600
For the big rain-soaked battle in "Noah,"

00:08:58.600 --> 00:09:01.250
the team had to create
an apocalyptic deluge

00:09:01.250 --> 00:09:03.530
on a literally biblical scale.

00:09:03.530 --> 00:09:04.780
But it's surprisingly hard

00:09:04.780 --> 00:09:07.340
to make rain show up well on camera.

00:09:07.340 --> 00:09:10.090
Plus, the crew would be
filming on an outdoor ark set

00:09:10.090 --> 00:09:12.780
about the size of two football fields.

00:09:12.780 --> 00:09:13.920
To cover all this ground,

00:09:13.920 --> 00:09:16.700
they came up with a
giant custom rain machine

00:09:16.700 --> 00:09:19.743
made up of a vast system of rain bars.

00:09:19.743 --> 00:09:22.127
Over 3,000 feet of pipes carried water

00:09:22.127 --> 00:09:24.000
from 100,000-gallon tanks

00:09:24.000 --> 00:09:26.094
into an array of rain sprinklers.

00:09:26.094 --> 00:09:29.020
One type of sprinkler released
smaller sprays of mist,

00:09:29.020 --> 00:09:31.410
while another produced larger droplets;

00:09:31.410 --> 00:09:33.940
they called those
sprinklers "goose drowners."

00:09:33.940 --> 00:09:35.869
So, massive amounts of rain?

00:09:35.869 --> 00:09:37.086
[rain pouring]
Check.

00:09:37.086 --> 00:09:39.010
Noah: Speak to me!

00:09:39.010 --> 00:09:40.720
Narrator: The complex
system made it possible

00:09:40.720 --> 00:09:43.846
for the team to dump 5,000
gallons of rain a minute

00:09:43.846 --> 00:09:46.169
on about 500 extras.

00:09:46.169 --> 00:09:47.930
Over two weeks of shooting,

00:09:47.930 --> 00:09:51.281
that added up to over a
million gallons of water,

00:09:51.281 --> 00:09:54.633
roughly as much as two
Olympic-sized swimming pools.

00:09:54.633 --> 00:09:57.050
Still, the filmmakers had
to make all that water

00:09:57.050 --> 00:09:58.961
translate well on screen,

00:09:58.961 --> 00:10:01.180
which had everything to do with lighting.

00:10:01.180 --> 00:10:03.862
Special effects artists
have a general rule:

00:10:03.862 --> 00:10:06.470
"Front-light snow, backlight rain."

00:10:06.470 --> 00:10:08.526
The deluge sequence was supposed to be set

00:10:08.526 --> 00:10:09.680
during the daytime,

00:10:09.680 --> 00:10:10.900
but if they filmed during the day

00:10:10.900 --> 00:10:13.254
the sun would constantly be moving,

00:10:13.254 --> 00:10:16.747
making it impossible to
consistently backlight the rain.

00:10:16.747 --> 00:10:19.140
So the team shot exclusively at night,

00:10:19.140 --> 00:10:22.230
using huge balloon space lights
on the rain-bar structure

00:10:22.230 --> 00:10:24.638
to light the area without shadows.

00:10:24.638 --> 00:10:26.710
They might've filmed in
the pitch-black night,

00:10:26.710 --> 00:10:27.985
but in the final scene,

00:10:27.985 --> 00:10:30.943
you can make out every drop of rain.

00:10:33.826 --> 00:10:36.160
To create the tornadoes
in "Into the Storm,"

00:10:36.160 --> 00:10:38.910
filmmakers did as much as
they could practically,

00:10:38.910 --> 00:10:41.290
with giant wind blowers, real tree parts,

00:10:41.290 --> 00:10:42.980
and massive rain machines.

00:10:42.980 --> 00:10:45.423
They even dropped real
cars out of the sky,

00:10:46.307 --> 00:10:48.151
"Fast and Furious" style.

00:10:48.151 --> 00:10:50.324
But an effect they couldn't do practically

00:10:50.324 --> 00:10:52.095
was the fire tornado.

00:10:56.700 --> 00:10:57.533
Believe it or not,

00:10:57.533 --> 00:10:59.730
firenados do actually happen in real life,

00:10:59.730 --> 00:11:00.818
but it's a phenomenon

00:11:00.818 --> 00:11:03.080
that could easily look fake in a movie.

00:11:03.080 --> 00:11:05.420
So the crew lit an actual fire on set

00:11:05.420 --> 00:11:07.930
when filming the original
photography for the scene.

00:11:07.930 --> 00:11:10.100
This provided a reference
for how the firenado

00:11:10.100 --> 00:11:12.280
would realistically light up the actors

00:11:12.280 --> 00:11:13.860
and surrounding structures.

00:11:13.860 --> 00:11:16.390
The digital artists then
applied appropriate lighting

00:11:16.390 --> 00:11:18.670
to CG structures, like this church.

00:11:18.670 --> 00:11:21.762
That glow on the sidewalk
and on the church's facade?

00:11:21.762 --> 00:11:24.770
A digital add-on that
seamlessly matched the footage.

00:11:24.770 --> 00:11:26.860
What wasn't a digital
add-on was the moment

00:11:26.860 --> 00:11:30.200
where a character gets sucked
into the firenado and killed.

00:11:30.200 --> 00:11:31.850
The team actually filmed that moment

00:11:31.850 --> 00:11:34.004
with a stuntman attached to a harness.

00:11:34.004 --> 00:11:37.087
[stuntman screaming]

00:11:43.052 --> 00:11:45.750
"The Day After Tomorrow"
had to depict a superstorm

00:11:45.750 --> 00:11:48.710
that looked like it could
bring on the next ice age,

00:11:48.710 --> 00:11:51.600
but ice and frost are
notoriously difficult

00:11:51.600 --> 00:11:53.150
to depict realistically.

00:11:53.150 --> 00:11:54.590
And many of the sequences set

00:11:54.590 --> 00:11:56.720
in an extreme arctic environment

00:11:56.720 --> 00:11:59.140
were impossible to pull off practically,

00:11:59.140 --> 00:12:01.070
like this scene, when
Dennis Quaid's character

00:12:01.070 --> 00:12:02.217
walks with his colleagues

00:12:02.217 --> 00:12:04.710
across the snow-covered
roof of a shopping mall.

00:12:04.710 --> 00:12:06.370
To safely depict the roof cracking

00:12:06.370 --> 00:12:07.580
under the characters' weight,

00:12:07.580 --> 00:12:09.910
the crew built miniature
models of the mall.

00:12:09.910 --> 00:12:12.290
They composited blue-screen
shots of the actors

00:12:12.290 --> 00:12:13.841
with shots of the model,

00:12:13.841 --> 00:12:16.260
then added a mix of
practical snow elements

00:12:16.260 --> 00:12:17.835
and CG particle snow

00:12:17.835 --> 00:12:20.343
to make the whole scene look frozen over.

